    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-elector-of-brandenburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-elector-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="John Sigismund, Elector of Brandenburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-elector-of-brandenburg">John Sigismund, Elector of Brandenburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Sigismund_Grunewald.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonymous / Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Prussia was created from the lands that the Hohenzollern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> had gathered over the previous century. In 1618 Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-elector-of-brandenburg">John Sigismund</a> inherited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a>, the secularised territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, which was held as a fief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William, the Great Elector</a> (r. 1640–1688), won full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wehlau">Treaty of Wehlau</a> in 1657 and built a standing army and a central tax administration across scattered domains that stretched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. His son, Elector Frederick III, wanted a royal crown to match the elevation of the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> to the Polish throne and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england">William of Orange</a> to the English one. Because no king other than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a> could exist within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, the crown was based on Prussia, which lay outside it, and Emperor Leopold I agreed in return for military support in the coming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. On 18 January 1701 Frederick crowned himself King in Prussia at Königsberg, becoming Frederick I. The title said ‘in’ rather than ‘of’ Prussia because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-prussia">West Prussia</a> was still Polish, but it gradually came to name the whole Hohenzollern state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berlin.jpg" alt="Berlin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Museumsinsel_Berlin_Juli_2021_1_(cropped)_b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kasa Fue; derivative work by Georgfotoart, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Prussia was a poor, thinly populated state of sandy soils, and its rulers treated economic policy as an instrument of power. East of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junkers">Junker</a> landlords held estates worked by dependent peasants under the manorial system known as <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutsherrschaft">Gutsherrschaft</a></em>, and the nobility supplied the officer corps, while townspeople paid the excise. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William</a> I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-grand-duke-of-baden">Frederick II</a> settled colonists, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> welcomed since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-potsdam">Edict of Potsdam</a> of 1685, drained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oderbruch">Oderbruch</a> marshes, built canals and promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, textiles and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a>. Religious toleration was state policy: the dynasty was Calvinist in a largely Lutheran land, and Silesia added a large Catholic population. Frederick William I decreed compulsory schooling in 1717, and Frederick II issued the General School Regulation of 1763. After 1807 the October Edict abolished hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, the reforms opened the trades, and an edict of 1812 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> citizens. In the nineteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, gained in 1815, made Prussia an industrial power, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and iron in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-silesia">Upper Silesia</a>, firms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a>, a railway network from the 1840s and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollverein">Zollverein</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a>, launched under Prussian leadership in 1834, which bound much of the German economy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuthen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuthen.jpg" alt="Battle of Leuthen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuthen">Battle of Leuthen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Leuthen_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carl Röchling, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The army was the core of the state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William</a> I, the ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-i-of-prussia">Soldier King</a>’ (r. 1713–1740), raised it to about 80,000 men, organised recruitment through the canton system of 1733, which assigned each regiment a district, and drilled it to unusual precision while leaving a full treasury. Frederick II used it at once: he invaded Silesia in 1740, won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mollwitz">Mollwitz</a> in 1741 and kept the province through the First and Second Silesian Wars. In the Seven Years’ War (1756–1763) Prussia, backed only by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, winning at Rossbach and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuthen">Leuthen</a> in 1757 but suffering crushing defeats such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kunersdorf">Kunersdorf</a> in 1759; it survived when Russia left the war after Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-pomerania">Elizabeth</a> died in 1762. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> destroyed the Frederician army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jena-auerstedt">Jena and Auerstedt</a> in 1806. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Gerhard von Scharnhorst</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-von-gneisenau">August von Gneisenau</a> rebuilt it with a general staff, promotion by merit and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landwehr">Landwehr</a> militia, and Prussian troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher</a> fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1813 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> in 1815. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmuth-von-moltke-the-elder">Helmuth von Moltke the Elder</a>, using railways, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> and the breech-loading needle gun, Prussia defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1864, Austria at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-koniggratz">Battle of Königgrätz</a> in 1866 and France at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sedan-1870">Battle of Sedan</a> in 1870.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/general-state-laws-for-the-prussian-states"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/general-state-laws-for-the-prussian-states.jpg" alt="General State Laws for the Prussian States" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/general-state-laws-for-the-prussian-states">General State Laws for the Prussian States</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Legal_systems_in_1895_Germany.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Borysk5, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Prussia was an absolute monarchy built on a centralised bureaucracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William</a> I created the General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-directory">Directory</a> in 1723 to run finance, war and the royal domains and recruited officials trained in cameralist administration, while the provincial estates kept their forms but lost most of their power. Frederick II, who called himself the first servant of the state, governed from his cabinet through written orders, restricted judicial torture on his accession and ordered the codification that became the General State Law of 1794 (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-state-laws-for-the-prussian-states">Allgemeines Landrecht</a></em>). After 1806 the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-friedrich-karl-vom-stein">Karl vom Stein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-august-von-hardenberg">Karl August von Hardenberg</a> created ministries, municipal self-government and a freer economy. The revolution of 1848 led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia">Frederick William IV</a> to grant a constitution, revised in 1850, which set up a parliament, the Landtag, whose lower house was elected by a three-class franchise weighted by taxes paid. In the constitutional conflict over army reform, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>, minister-president from 1862, governed for four years without an approved budget. After 1871 the King of Prussia was also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a>, the Prussian minister-president was usually the Imperial Chancellor, and Prussia held seventeen of the fifty-eight votes in the Federal Council (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundesrat-german-empire">Bundesrat</a></em>), enough to block constitutional change.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/konigsberg.jpg" alt="Königsberg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:K%C3%B6nigsberg_Castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Prussia’s rise turned German politics into a contest between two great powers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and Prussia, and its seizure of Silesia and share in the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partitions-of-poland">Partitions of Poland</a> (1772, 1793 and 1795) redrew the map of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-grand-duke-of-baden">Frederick II</a>, who corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> and wrote on politics, history and war, became the model of the enlightened absolutist. The reforms after 1806 included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-von-humboldt">Wilhelm von Humboldt</a>’s foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humboldt-university-of-berlin">University of Berlin</a> in 1810, which joined teaching to research and was imitated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and elsewhere; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a> taught at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-wilhelm-friedrich-hegel">G.W.F. Hegel</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. The general staff, short-service conscription with reserves, and officer training at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-war-academy">War Academy</a> in Berlin were copied by armies around the world after 1870. Above all, Prussia unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on its own terms, excluding Austria, through the wars of 1864–1871 and the proclamation of William I as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> on 18 January 1871. The new empire inherited Prussian traditions of bureaucracy and military power, and historians have long debated how far the political influence of the Prussian army and landowning elite contributed to the German catastrophes of the twentieth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-world-war.jpg" alt="First World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Verdun_1916.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Collection DocAnciens/docpix.fr, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Prussia was not conquered but absorbed into the empire it had created. After 1871 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> was both Prussian and imperial capital, and Prussian ministries were steadily overshadowed by imperial offices, although the three-class franchise kept conservative landowners powerful in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landtag-of-prussia">Prussian Landtag</a> to the end. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin">William II</a> promised franchise reform in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-controversy">Easter</a> message of 1917, but it was never enacted. Defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> brought revolution: on 9 November 1918 Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-maximilian-of-baden">Max von Baden</a> announced the abdication of William II, who fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and formally renounced the thrones of Prussia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 28 November. The monarchy was abolished, and Prussia became the Free State of Prussia, a republic within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>, governed for most of the 1920s by coalitions led by the Social Democrat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-braun">Otto Braun</a>. The government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-von-papen">Franz von Papen</a> deposed Braun’s cabinet in the coup of 20 July 1932 (the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-coup-of-1932">Preußenschlag</a></em>), and under Nazi rule Prussia lost any real self-government. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> its eastern provinces were transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and their German population expelled, and the Allied Control Council formally abolished Prussia by Law No. 46 of 25 February 1947.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Prussia turned the scattered Hohenzollern lands into a great power through an army and bureaucracy out of proportion to its size, became a model of enlightened absolutism and of educational and military reform, and in 1871 united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> under its king, shaping European history well into the twentieth century.</em></p>

